C = [
("h1", "4. “Delivered for Our Offences” and “Died for the Ungodly”"),
("p", "Claim: in the verses that follow Romans 3, Paul describes the death of Christ in language of persons: Christ was delivered for our offences, died for the ungodly, died for us while we were yet sinners, and reconciled us when we were enemies. The language is of love and of reconciliation, and the result is peace and the hope of being saved from wrath. The passages do not repeat the propitiation of 3:25. They add a different set of words for a different question."),
("sub", "4.1 “Delivered for our offences” (Romans 4:25)"),
("p", "Romans 4 ends with a verse that describes Christ in a paired clause: “Who was delivered for our offences, and was raised again for our justification” (Romans 4:25). The verse has two halves. He was delivered for our offences, and he was raised again for our justification. The two “for” clauses are not the same word in the same sense, and the verse does not say that the offences were removed in the first half and the justification given in the second, as though the work were divided. It says that each of the two events stands with a result."),
("p", "The verse closes the chapter on Abraham. Paul has said that righteousness was imputed to Abraham on the ground of believing God, and that it shall be imputed to us also, “if we believe on him that raised up Jesus our Lord from the dead” (Romans 4:24). Romans 4:25 then names the Lord Jesus who was delivered and raised. The sentence is the hinge between the example and the application, and it states the event the believing is about."),
("sub", "4.2 “Christ died for the ungodly” (Romans 5:6–8)"),
("p", "“For when we were yet without strength, in due time Christ died for the ungodly. For scarcely for a righteous man will one die: yet peradventure for a good man some would even dare to die. But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us” (Romans 5:6–8). The passage is an argument from the less to the greater. Men hardly die for a righteous man, though for a good man some might dare. Christ died for the ungodly, for sinners. The result drawn is that God commends his love."),
("tbl", "Romans 5:6–11, verse by verse.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 5:6", "“Christ died for the ungodly”", "The persons are ungodly and without strength; the time is due time"),
    ("Rom 5:8", "“while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us”", "The persons are sinners; the death is said to commend God’s love"),
    ("Rom 5:9", "“being now justified by his blood, we shall be saved from wrath through him”", "Justified by his blood; saved from wrath, in the future"),
    ("Rom 5:10", "“when we were enemies, we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son”", "Enemies reconciled by the death of the Son"),
    ("Rom 5:10", "“much more, being reconciled, we shall be saved by his life”", "A further result, by his life"),
    ("Rom 5:11", "“we also joy in God through our Lord Jesus Christ, by whom we have now received the atonement”", "Joy in God; atonement received now"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "4.3 “Enemies” and “reconciled”"),
("p", "The passage uses two new words for the persons and the result. The persons are “enemies” (Romans 5:10), and the result is that they are “reconciled to God by the death of his Son.” The word “atonement” in verse 11 is the King James rendering of the same Greek family as “reconciled,” and the volume reads it as the translators placed it: the believers have now received the reconciliation, and the Old Testament sense of the English word is not read into it. The study on reconciliation carries the full account of the word. This volume asks only what the Cross is said to do here, and the answer is that by the death of the Son, those who were enemies were reconciled."),
("p", "The tense of the verbs is part of the answer. “We were reconciled” is past. “We shall be saved” is future. “We have now received” is present. The passage places the death of Christ at the root of a past reconciliation, a present reception and a future salvation from wrath. It does not say that the future salvation is by the death. It says it is “by his life.” The volume leaves the distinction as Paul draws it."),
("sub", "4.4 What the passage does not say"),
("p", "Romans 5:6–11 does not say that Christ died as a propitiation in these verses. It does not say that his blood is the price. It does not use the word curse. It does not use the word sacrifice. The vocabulary here is of love, of reconciliation after enmity, and of salvation from wrath. In 3:25 the vocabulary was of the righteousness of God and the place where he set Christ forth. The two passages are in one letter and in neighbouring chapters, and they are not the same sentence. A reader who wants a system will be tempted to put them together. The volume asks that they be read separately first."),
("sub", "4.5 The persons named"),
("p", "The persons in these verses are described in Paul’s own terms: without strength, ungodly, sinners, enemies. The “us” is the writer and the readers of the letter. The passage does not say that Christ died for every person in the same words, and it does not say that he died only for those who believe. It says that he died for the ungodly and for us. Paul says in the same chapter that the “all men” of Adam’s line are reached by death (Romans 5:12), and Volume 1 reads that. The extent of the death of Christ is a question the next chapters touch again with the words “for all” (2 Corinthians 5:14, 15), and the volume handles it clause by clause."),
]
